      "speaker": {
        "id": "quince",
        "name": "QUINCE",
        "displayName": "Quince"
      },
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-line-0040",
          "number": 40,
          "speechLineNumber": 1,
          "text": "You may do it extempore, for it is nothing but roaring.",
          "plainText": "You may do it extempore for it is nothing but roaring",
          "lineType": "prose",
          "lineTypeDetection": "auto",
          "lineTypeConfidence": "medium",
          "lineTypeReason": "single-line speech block"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-speech-030",
      "type": "speech",
      "speaker": {
        "id": "bottom",
        "name": "BOTTOM",
        "displayName": "Bottom"
      },
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-line-0041",
          "number": 41,
          "speechLineNumber": 1,
          "text": "Let me play the lion too. I will roar that I will do any man's heart good to hear me. I will roar that I will make the Duke say 'Let him roar again, let him roar again.'",
          "plainText": "Let me play the lion too I will roar that I will do any man's heart good to hear me I will roar that I will make the Duke say 'Let him roar again let him roar again'",
          "lineType": "prose",
          "lineTypeDetection": "auto",
          "lineTypeConfidence": "medium",
          "lineTypeReason": "single-line speech block"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-speech-031",
      "type": "speech",
      "speaker": {
        "id": "quince",
        "name": "QUINCE",
        "displayName": "Quince"
      },
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-line-0042",
          "number": 42,
          "speechLineNumber": 1,
          "text": "If you should do it too terribly, you would fright the Duchess and the ladies, that they would shriek; and that were enough to hang us all.",
          "plainText": "If you should do it too terribly you would fright the Duchess and the ladies that they would shriek and that were enough to hang us all",
          "lineType": "prose",
          "lineTypeDetection": "auto",
          "lineTypeConfidence": "medium",
          "lineTypeReason": "single-line speech block"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-speech-032",
      "type": "speech",
      "speaker": {
        "id": "all",
        "name": "ALL",
        "displayName": "All"
      },
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-line-0043",
          "number": 43,
          "speechLineNumber": 1,
          "text": "That would hang us every mother's son.",
          "plainText": "That would hang us every mother's son",
          "lineType": "prose",
          "lineTypeDetection": "auto",
          "lineTypeConfidence": "medium",
          "lineTypeReason": "single-line speech block"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-speech-033",
      "type": "speech",
      "speaker": {
        "id": "bottom",
        "name": "BOTTOM",
        "displayName": "Bottom"
      },
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-line-0044",
          "number": 44,
          "speechLineNumber": 1,
          "text": "I grant you, friends, if you should fright the ladies out of their wits, they would have no more discretion but to hang us. But I will aggravate my voice so, that I will roar you as gently as any sucking dove; I will roar you an 'twere any nightingale.",
          "plainText": "I grant you friends if you should fright the ladies out of their wits they would have no more discretion but to hang us But I will aggravate my voice so that I will roar you as gently as any sucking dove I will roar you an 'twere any nightingale",
          "lineType": "prose",
          "lineTypeDetection": "auto",
          "lineTypeConfidence": "medium",
          "lineTypeReason": "single-line speech block"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-speech-034",
      "type": "speech",
      "speaker": {
        "id": "quince",
        "name": "QUINCE",
        "displayName": "Quince"
      },
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-line-0045",
          "number": 45,
          "speechLineNumber": 1,
          "text": "You can play no part but Pyramus, for Pyramus is a sweet-faced man; a proper man as one shall see in a summer's day; a most lovely gentleman-like man. Therefore you must needs play Pyramus.",
          "plainText": "You can play no part but Pyramus for Pyramus is a sweetfaced man a proper man as one shall see in a summer's day a most lovely gentlemanlike man Therefore you must needs play Pyramus",
          "lineType": "prose",
          "lineTypeDetection": "auto",
          "lineTypeConfidence": "medium",
          "lineTypeReason": "single-line speech block"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-speech-035",
      "type": "speech",
      "speaker": {
        "id": "bottom",
        "name": "BOTTOM",
        "displayName": "Bottom"
      },
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-line-0046",
          "number": 46,
          "speechLineNumber": 1,
          "text": "Well, I will undertake it. What beard were I best to play it in?",
          "plainText": "Well I will undertake it What beard were I best to play it in",
          "lineType": "prose",
          "lineTypeDetection": "auto",
          "lineTypeConfidence": "medium",
          "lineTypeReason": "single-line speech block"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-speech-036",
      "type": "speech",
      "speaker": {
        "id": "quince",
        "name": "QUINCE",
        "displayName": "Quince"
      },
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-line-0047",
          "number": 47,
          "speechLineNumber": 1,
          "text": "Why, what you will.",
          "plainText": "Why what you will",
          "lineType": "prose",
          "lineTypeDetection": "auto",
          "lineTypeConfidence": "medium",
          "lineTypeReason": "single-line speech block"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-speech-037",
      "type": "speech",
      "speaker": {
        "id": "bottom",
        "name": "BOTTOM",
        "displayName": "Bottom"
      },
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-line-0048",
          "number": 48,
          "speechLineNumber": 1,
          "text": "I will discharge it in either your straw-colour beard, your orange-tawny beard, your purple-in-grain beard, or your French-crown-colour beard, your perfect yellow.",
          "plainText": "I will discharge it in either your strawcolour beard your orangetawny beard your purpleingrain beard or your Frenchcrowncolour beard your perfect yellow",
          "lineType": "prose",
          "lineTypeDetection": "auto",
          "lineTypeConfidence": "medium",
          "lineTypeReason": "single-line speech block"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-speech-038",
      "type": "speech",
      "speaker": {
        "id": "quince",
        "name": "QUINCE",
        "displayName": "Quince"
      },
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-line-0049",
          "number": 49,
          "speechLineNumber": 1,
          "text": "Some of your French crowns have no hair at all, and then you will play bare-faced. But, masters, here are your parts, and I am to entreat you, request you, and desire you, to con them by tomorrow night; and meet me in the palace wood, a mile without the town, by moonlight; there will we rehearse, for if we meet in the city, we shall be dogg'd with company, and our devices known. In the meantime I will draw a bill of properties, such as our play wants. I pray you fail me not.",
          "plainText": "Some of your French crowns have no hair at all and then you will play barefaced But masters here are your parts and I am to entreat you request you and desire you to con them by tomorrow night and meet me in the palace wood a mile without the town by moonlight there will we rehearse for if we meet in the city we shall be dogg'd with company and our devices known In the meantime I will draw a bill of properties such as our play wants I pray you fail me not",
          "lineType": "prose",
          "lineTypeDetection": "auto",
          "lineTypeConfidence": "medium",
          "lineTypeReason": "single-line speech block"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-speech-039",
      "type": "speech",
      "speaker": {
        "id": "bottom",
        "name": "BOTTOM",
        "displayName": "Bottom"
      },
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "a-midsummer-nights-dream-1-2-line-0050",
          "number": 50,
          "speechLineNumber": 1,
          "text": "We will meet, and there we may rehearse most obscenely and courageously. Take pains, be perfect; adieu.",
          "plainText": "We will meet and there we may rehearse most obscenely and courageously Take pains be perfect adieu",
          "lineType": "prose",
          "lineTypeDetection": "auto",
          "lineTypeConfidence": "medium",
          "lineTypeReason": "single-line speech block"
        }
